    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All changes are complete and the test suite is green (`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the structured address form for `POST /api/owners` while keeping the flat `address` input backward-compatible.\n\n**Contract (`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`)**\n- Added `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` to `OwnerFields` (both optional; `addressLine2` genuinely optional). These flow into the generated `OwnerFieldsDto`/`OwnerDto`, so `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` are also returned.\n- Removed `address` from the schema's `required` list so a structured-only owner (no flat `address`) isn't rejected by bean validation's `@NotNull` before reaching the app logic. Required-ness is now enforced conditionally by the existing validator.\n\n**Persistence**\n- `Owner` model: added `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` columns + accessors (MapStruct auto-maps them by name).\n- Added `address_line1`/`address_line2` columns to all four schema variants (h2, hsqldb \u2014 the one the tests actually use, mysql, postgres).\n\n**Logic (new small unit, single responsibility)**\n- `AddressFormNormalizer` reconciles the two forms: it reuses the existing `AddressNormalizer` to normalize whichever fields are supplied, prefers the structured form when `addressLine1` is non-blank, and composes the flat `address` as `normalized(addressLine1)` plus a single space and `normalized(addressLine2)` when line 2 is present. Otherwise it falls back to normalizing the flat `address`.\n- The controller now calls this normalizer before validation. Because the composed `address` is non-blank exactly when a non-blank `addressLine1` **or** flat `address` was supplied, the existing `OwnerRequestValidator` (`address` + `city` required) naturally enforces the \"address in either form, plus city\" rule, and every downstream consumer reads the one normalized, composed address.\n\nI reused `AddressNormalizer` rather than duplicating its whitespace/upper-case/abbreviation rules, and kept it focused on single-string normalization while the new component owns only the form-reconciliation concern.",
